1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,320
Imagine that tomorrow, we
somehow prove that God exists.

2
00:00:04,149 --> 00:00:06,429
Not just find an unexplained event.

3
00:00:06,989 --> 00:00:09,549
Not discover something
strange in the universe.

4
00:00:10,129 --> 00:00:11,949
Not find an ancient text.

5
00:00:12,697 --> 00:00:16,397
Imagine we actually
establish it. God exists.

6
00:00:17,318 --> 00:00:19,958
At first, that sounds like
the end of the question.

7
00:00:20,038 --> 00:00:21,718
But it isn't.

8
00:00:21,798 --> 00:00:25,258
Because we have immediately
created another question.

9
00:00:26,373 --> 00:00:28,493
What exactly have we proved exists?

10
00:00:31,083 --> 00:00:36,063
Suppose we discover that the universe
has a cause. That would be extraordinary.

11
00:00:36,641 --> 00:00:41,101
But a cause of the universe is not
necessarily the God most people imagine.

12
00:00:41,361 --> 00:00:43,741
It could be something very different.

13
00:00:44,161 --> 00:00:45,881
So let's grant that much.

14
00:00:46,561 --> 00:00:50,301
Let's say there really is something
behind the existence of our universe.

15
00:00:51,065 --> 00:00:53,405
Something we might reasonably call God.

16
00:00:53,805 --> 00:00:56,065
What can we actually say about it?

17
00:00:58,075 --> 00:01:00,875
The first strange
question is: Where is God?

18
00:01:01,384 --> 00:01:03,624
If God created the universe,
it would be tempting

19
00:01:03,854 --> 00:01:06,924
to imagine God sitting
somewhere outside it.

20
00:01:07,064 --> 00:01:10,544
But what does "outside
the universe" even mean?

21
00:01:11,125 --> 00:01:13,025
The universe isn't necessarily a giant

22
00:01:13,255 --> 00:01:16,545
object floating inside
a larger empty space.

23
00:01:16,845 --> 00:01:19,605
Space itself is part of the universe.

24
00:01:20,011 --> 00:01:22,731
So if God created space,
God wouldn't simply

25
00:01:22,961 --> 00:01:25,571
be located somewhere
beyond the edge of space.

26
00:01:26,293 --> 00:01:27,713
There might not be an edge.

27
00:01:27,793 --> 00:01:31,293
And asking where God is
might not even make sense.

28
00:01:32,045 --> 00:01:34,445
God wouldn't be somewhere over there.

29
00:01:34,565 --> 00:01:39,125
God would have to exist in some sense that
doesn't depend on the space we inhabit.

30
00:01:41,595 --> 00:01:44,475
And then we encounter an
even stranger question.

31
00:01:44,945 --> 00:01:46,475
When did God exist?

32
00:01:47,125 --> 00:01:50,165
If time itself began with
the universe, asking what

33
00:01:50,395 --> 00:01:52,885
happened before the
universe may not make sense.

34
00:01:53,642 --> 00:01:57,122
It's a little like asking: “What
is north of the North Pole?”

35
00:01:57,877 --> 00:01:59,857
The question sounds grammatical.

36
00:01:59,937 --> 00:02:03,917
But the coordinate system itself
may have reached its limit.

37
00:02:04,409 --> 00:02:09,349
So if God created time, perhaps God
isn't simply an extremely old being.

38
00:02:09,789 --> 00:02:13,309
Perhaps the idea of being
old doesn't apply at all.

39
00:02:13,873 --> 00:02:18,093
God wouldn't have existed for a very
long time before creating the universe.

40
00:02:18,713 --> 00:02:20,333
There may have been no before.

41
00:02:21,117 --> 00:02:24,597
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

42
00:02:25,217 --> 00:02:26,837
It really helps the channel.

43
00:02:29,007 --> 00:02:31,267
Now ask something even more basic.

44
00:02:31,807 --> 00:02:33,307
What is God made of?

45
00:02:33,884 --> 00:02:40,144
Everything we normally encounter is made
of something. Atoms. Particles. Fields.

46
00:02:40,224 --> 00:02:41,424
Radiation.

47
00:02:41,899 --> 00:02:45,119
Even seemingly empty space
has physical properties.

48
00:02:45,705 --> 00:02:48,965
But if God is the creator of
the physical universe, saying

49
00:02:49,195 --> 00:02:52,525
that God is made of ordinary
matter would create a problem.

50
00:02:52,605 --> 00:02:55,685
That matter would already have to exist.

51
00:02:56,149 --> 00:03:00,689
So classical concepts of God often
describe God as non-physical.

52
00:03:01,129 --> 00:03:02,869
But now we have another problem.

53
00:03:03,564 --> 00:03:07,364
We have spent our entire lives
experiencing physical things.

54
00:03:07,924 --> 00:03:11,844
We don't actually have an intuitive
category for a non-physical object.

55
00:03:12,579 --> 00:03:16,679
We can say the words. But what
would such a thing actually be?

56
00:03:19,039 --> 00:03:22,539
And this is where the idea
becomes particularly interesting.

57
00:03:23,339 --> 00:03:27,699
Suppose a cosmological argument
successfully establishes a first cause.

58
00:03:28,439 --> 00:03:32,939
What have we learned? Perhaps:
Something caused the universe to exist.

59
00:03:33,721 --> 00:03:37,281
But how do we get from that to:
Someone caused the universe?

60
00:03:38,105 --> 00:03:41,125
A cause doesn't necessarily
have to be a person.

61
00:03:41,805 --> 00:03:43,945
A rock can cause a window to break.

62
00:03:44,667 --> 00:03:46,987
A star can cause a planet to orbit.

63
00:03:47,647 --> 00:03:50,727
A physical process can cause
another physical process.

64
00:03:51,206 --> 00:03:53,866
So why would the ultimate
cause have a mind?

65
00:03:54,566 --> 00:03:59,706
One possibility is that a personal cause
is required for some philosophical reason.

66
00:03:59,866 --> 00:04:02,826
But that requires an additional argument.

67
00:04:03,581 --> 00:04:06,981
The word God doesn't automatically
provide the missing steps.

68
00:04:09,391 --> 00:04:14,751
The major monotheistic religions
describe God as conscious. God knows.

69
00:04:15,191 --> 00:04:19,051
God understands. God
chooses. God can respond.

70
00:04:19,497 --> 00:04:24,137
But consciousness, as far as we know, is
associated with physical nervous systems.

71
00:04:24,957 --> 00:04:27,497
So what would consciousness
without a brain mean?

72
00:04:28,086 --> 00:04:31,606
Maybe consciousness doesn't
fundamentally require a brain.

73
00:04:31,926 --> 00:04:35,886
Maybe our brains are simply one
way consciousness can exist.

74
00:04:36,311 --> 00:04:39,771
But at this point, we are trying
to extend concepts developed from

75
00:04:40,001 --> 00:04:43,351
human experience into a completely
different kind of existence.

76
00:04:43,993 --> 00:04:47,713
And if God has thoughts, do those
thoughts happen one after another?

77
00:04:48,313 --> 00:04:50,693
Because if they do, that sounds like time.

78
00:04:51,045 --> 00:04:53,845
One thought happens. Then
another. Then another.

79
00:04:54,290 --> 00:04:58,970
But if God exists outside time, perhaps
divine thought doesn't work like ours.

80
00:04:59,410 --> 00:05:02,070
Maybe there is no sequence of thoughts.

81
00:05:02,836 --> 00:05:06,216
No waiting. No changing
from one thought to another.

82
00:05:06,476 --> 00:05:09,716
Instead, God might somehow
know things all at once.

83
00:05:10,122 --> 00:05:11,522
That sounds strange.

84
00:05:11,622 --> 00:05:15,982
But we shouldn't reject an idea merely
because it's difficult to imagine.

85
00:05:16,769 --> 00:05:20,529
Our inability to picture something
isn't proof that it is impossible.

86
00:05:21,409 --> 00:05:25,809
It may simply mean that our brains evolved
to understand the world at human scale.

87
00:05:28,509 --> 00:05:32,269
Now suppose we have established
some kind of transcendent creator.

88
00:05:32,809 --> 00:05:35,269
Does that tell us there is only one?

89
00:05:35,839 --> 00:05:37,179
Not necessarily.

90
00:05:37,859 --> 00:05:41,279
Our universe could, at least
conceptually, have multiple creators.

91
00:05:41,973 --> 00:05:44,713
If there are two or more of
them, perhaps they cooperate.

92
00:05:45,053 --> 00:05:47,113
Perhaps they disagree.

93
00:05:47,253 --> 00:05:51,913
Perhaps one created part of reality
and another created something else.

94
00:05:52,227 --> 00:05:54,227
And if we want to call
that being the God, rather

95
00:05:54,457 --> 00:05:57,267
than merely a god, we're
already adding information.

96
00:06:00,017 --> 00:06:03,697
What about the familiar idea
that God is all-powerful?

97
00:06:04,157 --> 00:06:06,557
This raises an old philosophical puzzle.

98
00:06:07,176 --> 00:06:11,716
Can an omnipotent God create a rock
so heavy that God cannot lift it?

99
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:16,460
If God can create it, then there is
something God cannot do: lift it.

100
00:06:16,681 --> 00:06:21,281
If God cannot create it, then there
is something God cannot do: create it.

101
00:06:21,855 --> 00:06:23,435
The puzzle sounds devastating.

102
00:06:23,895 --> 00:06:27,255
But it depends on what we
mean by “can do anything.”

103
00:06:27,990 --> 00:06:32,790
Perhaps omnipotence means the ability to
do anything that is logically possible.

104
00:06:33,526 --> 00:06:35,526
Under that definition, creating a square

105
00:06:35,756 --> 00:06:38,826
circle isn't a task God
fails to accomplish.

106
00:06:39,606 --> 00:06:42,346
It's not a coherent
task in the first place.

107
00:06:43,011 --> 00:06:44,911
The same could apply to the rock.

108
00:06:45,691 --> 00:06:47,851
The paradox may reveal
something about the meaning of

109
00:06:48,081 --> 00:06:51,951
omnipotence rather than a
contradiction in the existence of God.

110
00:06:54,551 --> 00:06:56,091
Now consider knowledge.

111
00:06:56,211 --> 00:06:59,611
If God knows everything,
then God knows the future.

112
00:07:00,130 --> 00:07:02,410
But what does that mean for human freedom?

113
00:07:03,090 --> 00:07:07,030
Suppose God knows with absolute
certainty what you will do tomorrow.

114
00:07:07,716 --> 00:07:09,656
Could you still choose differently?

115
00:07:10,700 --> 00:07:14,140
If you did choose differently, God's
knowledge would have been wrong.

116
00:07:14,580 --> 00:07:18,480
But if you cannot choose differently,
in what sense is the choice free?

117
00:07:19,139 --> 00:07:21,839
Philosophers have argued
about this for centuries.

118
00:07:22,679 --> 00:07:26,179
Some say divine foreknowledge
is compatible with free will.

119
00:07:26,468 --> 00:07:28,908
Others think there is a genuine conflict.

120
00:07:29,588 --> 00:07:34,288
And some reject the idea that the future
contains one fixed set of facts at all.

121
00:07:36,748 --> 00:07:41,828
And then we reach perhaps the most
important property of all. Is God good?

122
00:07:42,363 --> 00:07:45,463
If we discover that some
creator exists, that alone

123
00:07:45,693 --> 00:07:48,903
wouldn't tell us that this
creator is morally perfect.

124
00:07:49,563 --> 00:07:52,483
A powerful creator could
theoretically be indifferent.

125
00:07:52,863 --> 00:07:55,583
Or morally strange. Or hostile.

126
00:07:55,913 --> 00:07:57,683
Or simply unlike us.

127
00:07:57,961 --> 00:08:02,301
If we want to conclude that God is
perfectly good, we need another reason.

128
00:08:03,081 --> 00:08:06,781
As we saw in an earlier video, this
raises the problem of suffering.

129
00:08:09,481 --> 00:08:14,981
And now we reach a question that I find
particularly strange. If God is perfect...

130
00:08:15,141 --> 00:08:17,181
does God actually need anything?

131
00:08:17,749 --> 00:08:21,229
Suppose God is complete.
God lacks nothing.

132
00:08:21,879 --> 00:08:23,279
God doesn't need food.

133
00:08:23,359 --> 00:08:25,119
Doesn't need shelter.

134
00:08:25,279 --> 00:08:28,559
Doesn't need money.
Doesn't need protection.

135
00:08:28,639 --> 00:08:30,299
Doesn't even need company.

136
00:08:30,663 --> 00:08:34,203
Then why would God want
anything? Why create a universe?

137
00:08:34,663 --> 00:08:36,343
Why create conscious beings?

138
00:08:37,184 --> 00:08:41,184
Was God lonely? That seems strange
for a being that needs nothing.

139
00:08:41,264 --> 00:08:43,944
Was God bored? Even stranger.

140
00:08:44,772 --> 00:08:49,952
Why would God care whether humans worship?
Why answer prayers? Why give commandments?

141
00:08:50,032 --> 00:08:51,292
Why judge anyone?

142
00:08:51,889 --> 00:08:55,029
Maybe creation wasn't
something God needed to do.

143
00:08:55,689 --> 00:08:58,029
Maybe it was something God chose to do.

144
00:08:58,279 --> 00:09:00,959
But then we have another question. Why?

145
00:09:03,903 --> 00:09:05,983
And this may be the central point.

146
00:09:06,543 --> 00:09:09,363
There is a huge difference
between these two statements:

147
00:09:10,081 --> 00:09:11,861
Something caused the universe.

148
00:09:12,694 --> 00:09:15,674
And: There is an all-powerful,
all-knowing, perfectly good,

149
00:09:15,904 --> 00:09:19,814
personal God who created the
universe, cares about humans,

150
00:09:19,914 --> 00:09:23,554
answers prayers, gives moral
commands, and wants to be known.

151
00:09:24,139 --> 00:09:28,619
The second statement contains an enormous
amount of additional information.

152
00:09:29,479 --> 00:09:31,139
Where did all of it come from?

153
00:09:31,619 --> 00:09:35,359
Not necessarily from the original
argument for a first cause.

154
00:09:36,159 --> 00:09:39,379
Each additional property
requires its own justification.

155
00:09:40,399 --> 00:09:42,379
Maybe those arguments exist.

156
00:09:42,659 --> 00:09:46,099
Maybe some are strong.
Maybe some are weak.

157
00:09:46,339 --> 00:09:48,059
But they are separate questions.

158
00:09:50,409 --> 00:09:52,229
There's a broader lesson here.

159
00:09:52,849 --> 00:09:55,309
Whenever we explain something,
we have to be careful

160
00:09:55,539 --> 00:09:58,549
not to smuggle extra information
into the explanation.

161
00:09:59,425 --> 00:10:01,945
Imagine finding an ancient machine.

162
00:10:02,585 --> 00:10:05,125
You determine that someone
must have built it.

163
00:10:05,345 --> 00:10:06,965
That tells you something.

164
00:10:07,305 --> 00:10:09,445
But it doesn't tell you who built it.

165
00:10:09,709 --> 00:10:13,149
What they looked like. Why they
built it. Whether they were kind.

166
00:10:13,379 --> 00:10:15,429
Or whether they even still exist.

167
00:10:16,292 --> 00:10:20,472
The evidence for one property doesn't
automatically establish all the others.

168
00:10:21,192 --> 00:10:23,272
The same reasoning applies to God.

169
00:10:25,692 --> 00:10:29,032
So what is God? A person? A mind? A force?

170
00:10:29,112 --> 00:10:30,652
The first cause of the universe?

171
00:10:31,332 --> 00:10:33,472
Something outside space and time?

172
00:10:34,048 --> 00:10:36,988
Perhaps the word “God”
is doing too much work.

173
00:10:37,668 --> 00:10:39,668
It may refer to very different ideas in

174
00:10:39,898 --> 00:10:42,228
different philosophical
and religious traditions.

175
00:10:42,806 --> 00:10:44,906
So asking: “Does God exist?”

176
00:10:45,706 --> 00:10:48,966
can sound like we're asking
about one clearly defined object.

177
00:10:49,431 --> 00:10:53,991
But perhaps we're actually asking about an
entire family of very different concepts.

178
00:10:56,621 --> 00:10:59,761
And there is one final
possibility worth considering.

179
00:11:00,381 --> 00:11:03,241
Maybe if we discovered
the ultimate foundation of

180
00:11:03,471 --> 00:11:06,961
reality, we wouldn't
immediately recognise it as God.

181
00:11:07,599 --> 00:11:09,279
It might not be a person.

182
00:11:09,859 --> 00:11:12,839
Its thinking might not
resemble human thought at all.

183
00:11:13,435 --> 00:11:18,435
It might not even be the kind of thing for
which the words “what is it?” make sense.

184
00:11:19,229 --> 00:11:22,349
We would have discovered something
fundamental about reality.

185
00:11:22,709 --> 00:11:26,949
But we might still be arguing about
whether it deserves to be called God.

186
00:11:29,909 --> 00:11:33,329
So perhaps the question
isn't simply: Does God exist?

187
00:11:34,208 --> 00:11:38,108
The deeper question is: What would
we actually mean by “God exists”?

188
00:11:38,819 --> 00:11:41,419
If we mean a first
cause, that's one claim.

189
00:11:42,079 --> 00:11:44,779
If we mean an intelligent
creator, that's another.

190
00:11:45,081 --> 00:11:49,121
If we mean a perfectly good being who
cares about humans and answers prayers...

191
00:11:49,501 --> 00:11:51,121
that's another claim again.

192
00:11:52,190 --> 00:11:55,790
So if someone ever proves that
God exists, don't stop there.

193
00:11:56,310 --> 00:11:57,890
Ask the next question.

194
00:11:59,096 --> 00:12:01,296
What exactly did they prove exists?

195
00:12:02,357 --> 00:12:04,777
Because proving that there is
something behind the universe...

196
00:12:05,007 --> 00:12:07,547
may be only the beginning of the mystery.
